  "result": "**The Memory Moths**\n\nI've been thinking about memory moths lately.\n\nI don't think they're real, but I wish they were. I imagine them very small and very pale, not like the bright moths that throw themselves at porch lights in summer, but quieter moths that come out in the blue hour, when the day is technically over but nobody has quite turned the lights on yet.\n\nThey don't live in closets or attics eating wool. They live in thresholds. In the pause between a question and an answer. In train stations, on the last page of notebooks people never quite finished, in kitchen drawers where rubber bands have fused into fossils. Memory moths settle, I think, on the edges of things.\n\nThey don't collect whole memories. That would be too heavy, and also rude. They collect the *crumbs* around them. The smell of a specific rain that you can't place to a year. The exact way your hands felt cold on a steering wheel. The sentence you almost said to someone you loved, in a caf\u00e9, in 2019, and then didn't. They gather these up one by one, on wings thin as tissue paper, and carry them very, very slowly to a place we can't see.\n\nSometimes, on a still evening, you might see one drift past your window. It won't flutter. It will just *be* there for a second, powdered with dust that isn't dust, and then be gone. If you open the window, it might land on the sill. The old advice, in the town where I first heard about them, was to leave it alone. Don't name what it's carrying. Just let it rest. It is probably tired.\n\nI like this theory because it explains so much that otherwise feels a little unkind. Why you suddenly remember a song from a car radio when you're forty and crying in a grocery store. Why your grandfather's voice comes back, clear as a bell, while you're tying your shoes. Why some afternoons feel full of people who aren't there anymore.\n\nMaybe we were never meant to keep everything. Maybe something small and gentle is keeping it for us, in the in-between light, until we're ready to write back.\n\nIf you see one tonight, let me know.",
